{"name":"Sea Cave","hex":"#005986","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sea cave","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A natural cavity formed in a cliff or rock face by the relentless wave action of the ocean or a large lake; carved out by continuous mechanical erosion (rather than inland caves formed by chemical dissolution)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sea_cave"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sea cave","blocks":[{"text":"A sea cave or littoral cave is a type of cave formed primarily by the wave action of the sea. The primary process involved is erosion. Sea caves are found throughout the world, actively forming along present coastlines and as relict sea caves on former coastlines. Some of the largest wave-cut caves in the world are found on the coast of Norway, but are now 100 feet or more above present sea level. These would still be classified as littoral caves. By contrast, in places like Thailand's Phang Nga Bay, solutionally formed caves in limestone have been flooded by the rising sea and are now subject to littoral erosion, representing a new phase of their enlargement."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Seacave_fault.jpg/330px-Seacave_fault.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sea_cave","wikidata":"Q1052919"},{"title":"Sea","blocks":[{"text":"A sea is a very large body of salty water. The term has two distinct meanings. The first is the interconnected body of seawater that spans most of Earth, known as the sea or the ocean. The second is a named body of water: usually either a marginal sea, or a large, nearly landlocked body of water."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Mediterranean_Sea_16.61811E_38.99124N.jpg/330px-Mediterranean_Sea_16.61811E_38.99124N.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sea","wikidata":"Q165"},{"title":"Cave","blocks":[{"text":"Caves and caverns are natural voids under the surface of the Earth and have also been observed in other rocky worlds. Caves often form by the weathering of rock and can extend deep underground. Exogene caves are smaller openings that extend a relatively short distance underground. Caves which extend farther underground than the opening is wide are called endogene caves."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/Lechuguilla_Cave_Pearlsian_Gulf.jpg/330px-Lechuguilla_Cave_Pearlsian_Gulf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cave","wikidata":"Q35509"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Littoral_sinkhole.jpg/500px-Littoral_sinkhole.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Littoral_sinkhole.jpg/500px-Littoral_sinkhole.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sea cave collapse","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALittoral_sinkhole.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Akun_Island_basalt_sea_cave.jpg/500px-Akun_Island_basalt_sea_cave.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Akun_Island_basalt_sea_cave.jpg/1280px-Akun_Island_basalt_sea_cave.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Akun_Island_basalt_sea_cave.jpg/500px-Akun_Island_basalt_sea_cave.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Akun Island basalt sea cave","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAkun_Island_basalt_sea_cave.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Seacaving.jpg/500px-Seacaving.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Seacaving.jpg/500px-Seacaving.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Exploring a sea cave","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASeacaving.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/BlueMarble-2001-2002.jpg/500px-BlueMarble-2001-2002.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/BlueMarble-2001-2002.jpg/1280px-BlueMarble-2001-2002.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/BlueMarble-2001-2002.jpg/500px-BlueMarble-2001-2002.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Composite images of the Earth created by NASA in 2001","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlueMarble-2001-2002.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Propagation_du_tsunami_en_profondeur_variable.gif/330px-Propagation_du_tsunami_en_profondeur_variable.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Propagation_du_tsunami_en_profondeur_variable.gif/330px-Propagation_du_tsunami_en_profondeur_variable.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"When the wave enters shallow water, it slows down and its amplitude (height) increases.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APropagation_du_tsunami_en_profondeur_variable.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/2004-tsunami.jpg/500px-2004-tsunami.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/2004-tsunami.jpg/